  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\nWe tackle the Erd\u0151s minimal overlap problem by implementing a physics-informed multi-resolution optimization framework that exploits the problem's connectivity structure. The key bottleneck is efficiently exploring solutions that minimize overlap at multiple scales. We introduce three innovations:\n- A derivative-free optimization using coordinate-wavelet transforms to handle integral constraints more effectively\n- A multi-projection strategy that adapts to the local curvature of the solution\n- A hierarchical coarse-to-fine resolution method that transfers patterns across scales using wavelet interpolation for improved performance\n\nThe approach combines continuous relaxation and randomized rounding, ensuring integral feasibility throughout optimization. The physics-inspired framework incorporates constraints directly into the optimization landscape using penalized gradients.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\nWe optimize a step function h(x) \u2208 [0,1] satisfying:\n- 0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1 \u2200i\n- \u03a3h_i = n_points/2\n- Minimize C5 = max_k ||h(x)(1-h(x+k))||_L1( dx )\n\nWe represent solutions using normalized B-splines (for smoothness) and use a continuous relaxation for optimization. The cost function is expressed via W1 Sobolev gradients to maintain smoothness. Constraints are enforced via:\n- Integral constraint: \u222bh(x)dx = n_points/2\n- Boundedness: 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1\n\nThe optimization formulates a generative model:\nf(h) = ||h||_2^2 (for smoothness) + \u03bb||\u222bh(x)dx - n/2||_1 + \u03bc\u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\nwhere \u03bb>0 and \u03bc>0 are regularization parameters.\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n1. Preprocessing:\n   - Validate input n_points with seed=42\n   - Initialize n=400 grid\n   - Scale to [0,2] interval\n\n2. Algorithm selection:\n   - Use trust-region-reflective method for nonlinear constrained optimization\n   - B-spline basis functions as default basis for smoothness\n   - Progressive resolution up from 400 to 800 to 1600 points\n\n3. Step-by-step execution:\n   - Phased execution: (1) Coarse grid initialization, (2) Local optimization, (3) Coarse-to-fine transfer\n   - Repair phase: Gradient projection with L1 norm penalty\n   - Optimization loop:\n        Initialize h(x) for grid points\n        Project constraints using integral preservation\n        Compute cost function via:\n            \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx over LAG bounds\n            dy/dx penalty terms for boundary values\n        Apply coordinate descent until convergence\n\n4. Search choices:\n   - Basis selection (B-splines, Fourier modes) per resolution\n   - Regularization parameter schedule (\u03bb=[5,2,1] for n=400,800,1600)\n   - Dimension reduction technique: Local coordinate selection\n\n5. Timing budget distribution:\n   - 40% to coarse grid optimization (400pts)\n   - 30% to intermediate grid (800pts)\n   - 30% to fine grid (1200pts)\n   - 10% to rounding and validation\n\n4. Search Choices and Useful Variations\nInitial parameters:\n   - Regularization \u03bc_0 = 0.5/(n_points/2)\n   - Lambda schedule \u03bc_k = \u03bc_0 / k (k resolution level)\n   - Basis adaptation: Basis width dynamically adjusted based on curvature\n\nProgressive steps:\n   - n=400: Basis order 3-8, evaluation points 1000 steps\n   - n=800: Basis order 4-9, evaluation points 2000 steps\n   - n=1600: Basis order 5-10, evaluation points 4000 steps\n\nConstraint handling variations:\n   - For integral constraint: Multi-point clamping with curvature penalty\n   - For bounds: Dual projection with slack variables\n   - For feasibility: Use Dykstra's projection with interval tracking\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness\nAlgorithm cost estimate:\n   - O((n_points\u2074)) for basis function evaluation\n   - Memory: \u22482*n_points*basis_order elements\n   - Primary cost: CVXPY problem formulation (\u223c0.2*n_points\u00b3)\n\nFeasibility checks:\n   - Initial: Project to L2-orthogonal complement\n   - Regular: Compute trust region price every iteration\n   - Final: Rounding with \u220a-feasibility check\n\nEarly termination rules:\n   - Compute \u0394 = C5(new) - C5(previous)\n   - If \u0394 > -1e-4 over 30 steps, reduce \u03bc by factor 0.9\n   - If C5(new) < 0.382 or \u03bc < 1e-7, terminate with current solution\n\nFallback mechanism:\n   - Interpolate finest solution to coarsest points\n   - Use local coordinate optimization to refine coarse solution\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\nPhase checking:\n   - Compute C5 using dyadic correlation with explicit dx\n   - Check: 0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1 for all i with \u227c1e-6 violation\n   - Final solution: Randomize 10% for diverse testing\n\nOutput format:\n   - h_values: Normalized [0,1] vector of length n\n   - c5_bound: Computed using exact integral method\n   - n_points: Grid size used in optimization\n\nSanity checks:\n   - Verify integral is within [n/2 - 1, n/2 + 1]\n   - Check correlation magnitude not exceeding max_hypothesized_bound\n   - Ensure C5 \u2264 expected the value (should be \u22640.38)\n\nReturn early if:\n   - Time < 120s and no improvement seen\n   - Last candidate outperforms parent by <0.99% reduction\n\nProvides a solid lower bound through:\n   - Multi-resolution assessment \n   - Progressive curvature estimation\n   - Trust-region enforcement",
